1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:16,500
[Host] Welcome to Japanese Daily News. Today is May twenty-fifth. Today's story: Tokyo might run its trains twenty-four hours a day. Right now, trains stop around midnight. That could change. Let's listen.

2
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:24,660
[News] 東京都は、電車を24時間走らせることを考えています。

3
00:00:25,359 --> 00:00:35,584
[News] 今、東京の電車は夜12時ごろに止まります。朝5時まで電車はありません。

4
00:00:36,283 --> 00:00:44,948
[News] 夜遅くまで働く人や、外国からの旅行者にとって、これは不便です。

5
00:00:45,648 --> 00:00:52,920
[News] まず、金曜日と土曜日の夜だけ、試してみる予定です。

6
00:00:53,619 --> 00:00:59,404
[News] 使う路線は、山手線と中央線です。

7
00:01:00,103 --> 00:01:08,695
[News] でも、夜の間に線路の点検をしているので、安全の問題があります。

8
00:01:09,396 --> 00:01:20,628
[News] 東京都の担当者は、「安全が一番大切です。よく調べてから決めます」と言いました。

9
00:01:21,328 --> 00:01:26,343
[Host] Okay. This is a short and simple story. Let's go through it.

10
00:01:26,944 --> 00:01:28,719
[Host] The first sentence:

11
00:01:29,319 --> 00:01:38,751
[Slow] 東京都は、電車を24時間走らせることを考えています。

12
00:01:39,552 --> 00:01:45,263
[Host] The Tokyo Metropolitan Government is thinking about running trains twenty-four hours a day.

13
00:01:45,864 --> 00:01:48,096
[Vocab] 電車

14
00:01:48,495 --> 00:01:54,760
[Host] Train. One of the first words you learn in Japanese. You'll use it every day in Tokyo.

15
00:01:55,359 --> 00:01:57,951
[Vocab] 走らせる

16
00:01:58,352 --> 00:02:06,751
[Host] To make something run. This is the causative form — the base verb means 'to run,' and this form means to make something run.

17
00:02:07,352 --> 00:02:09,943
[Vocab] 走らせる

18
00:02:10,343 --> 00:02:16,943
[Host] Don't worry about the grammar for now. Just remember: trains run, but people make trains run.

19
00:02:17,544 --> 00:02:20,424
[Vocab] 考えています

20
00:02:20,824 --> 00:02:27,256
[Host] Is thinking about, is considering. It means they are currently thinking about it — no decision yet.

21
00:02:27,855 --> 00:02:28,936
[Host] Next:

22
00:02:29,536 --> 00:02:37,360
[Slow] 今、東京の電車は夜12時ごろに止まります。

23
00:02:38,159 --> 00:02:47,711
[Host] Right now, Tokyo's trains stop around midnight. 'Yoru' means night. Simple, right? You can also say 'yonaka' for the middle of the night.

24
00:02:48,312 --> 00:02:50,231
[Vocab] ごろ

25
00:02:50,632 --> 00:02:54,256
[Host] Around, approximately — but only for time.

26
00:02:54,855 --> 00:02:57,015
[Vocab] 時ごろ

27
00:02:57,415 --> 00:03:02,936
[Host] Together it means around twelve o'clock. For amounts, you use 'gurai' instead.

28
00:03:03,536 --> 00:03:05,671
[Vocab] 止まる

29
00:03:06,072 --> 00:03:12,527
[Host] to stop. This is one of the most useful verbs for daily life in Japan. For example:

30
00:03:13,127 --> 00:03:16,895
[Slow] バスが止まりました。

31
00:03:17,695 --> 00:03:19,231
[Host] The bus stopped.

32
00:03:19,831 --> 00:03:21,848
[Host] Then we learn who needs this:

33
00:03:22,448 --> 00:03:33,271
[Slow] 夜遅くまで働く人や、外国からの旅行者にとって、これは不便です。

34
00:03:34,072 --> 00:03:39,711
[Host] For people who work late at night and travelers from abroad, this is inconvenient.

35
00:03:40,312 --> 00:03:42,759
[Vocab] 働く

36
00:03:43,159 --> 00:04:05,888
[Host] To work. A very common verb. You'll hear it in many news stories. 'Ryokousha' means traveler — 'ryokou' is travel and 'sha' means person. So it's a travel person — a traveler. 'Fuben' means inconvenient. The opposite is 'benri,' which means convenient. 'Fu' is a prefix that means 'not' — so not convenient.

37
00:04:06,487 --> 00:04:07,687
[Host] The plan:

38
00:04:08,288 --> 00:04:17,384
[Slow] まず、金曜日と土曜日の夜だけ、試してみる予定です。

39
00:04:18,184 --> 00:04:22,983
[Host] First, they plan to try it only on Friday and Saturday nights.

40
00:04:23,584 --> 00:04:25,624
[Vocab] まず

41
00:04:26,024 --> 00:04:30,608
[Host] First, to begin with. A great word for explaining steps.

42
00:04:31,208 --> 00:04:33,319
[Vocab] てみる

43
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:40,512
[Host] This ending means 'to try doing something.' When you add it to a verb, it means let's try it and see.

44
00:04:41,112 --> 00:04:43,295
[Vocab] 予定

45
00:04:43,696 --> 00:04:47,872
[Host] Plan, schedule. Very useful. For example:

46
00:04:48,471 --> 00:04:53,079
[Slow] 明日の予定は何ですか？

47
00:04:53,879 --> 00:04:55,896
[Host] What are your plans for tomorrow?

48
00:04:56,495 --> 00:04:58,439
[Host] Which lines will they use?

49
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:06,264
[Slow] 使う路線は、山手線と中央線です。

50
00:05:07,064 --> 00:05:17,432
[Host] The lines they'll use are the Yamanote Line and the Chuo Line. If you've been to Tokyo, you know these — the green circle line and the orange line. Train line, route.

51
00:05:18,031 --> 00:05:19,759
[Host] But there's a problem:

52
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:31,088
[Slow] でも、夜の間に線路の点検をしているので、安全の問題があります。

53
00:05:31,887 --> 00:05:36,351
[Host] But they do track inspections during the night, so there are safety concerns.

54
00:05:36,951 --> 00:05:39,112
[Vocab] 安全

55
00:05:39,512 --> 00:05:47,360
[Host] Safety. You'll see this word on signs everywhere in Japan. The opposite is 'kiken,' which means dangerous.

56
00:05:47,959 --> 00:05:50,216
[Vocab] 問題

57
00:05:50,615 --> 00:05:54,480
[Host] Problem, issue. Another word you'll hear constantly.

58
00:05:55,079 --> 00:05:57,216
[Host] And the official's comment:

59
00:05:57,815 --> 00:06:06,983
[Slow] 安全が一番大切です。よく調べてから決めます。

60
00:06:07,783 --> 00:06:24,103
[Host] Safety is the most important thing. We will investigate carefully before deciding. 'Ichiban' means number one, the most. 'Ichiban taisetsu' means the most important. 'Ichiban suki' means favorite. Very useful pattern.

61
00:06:24,704 --> 00:06:27,151
[Vocab] 大切

62
00:06:27,552 --> 00:06:37,247
[Host] Important, precious. A warm word — you use it for things you truly care about. The next key word means 'to decide.' For example:

63
00:06:37,848 --> 00:06:41,687
[Slow] まだ決めていません。

64
00:06:42,487 --> 00:06:46,375
[Host] I haven't decided yet. A phrase you'll use a lot.

65
00:06:46,975 --> 00:06:49,975
[Host] Let's listen to the full story one more time.

66
00:06:50,576 --> 00:06:58,136
[News] 東京都は、電車を24時間走らせることを考えています。

67
00:06:58,836 --> 00:07:09,060
[News] 今、東京の電車は夜12時ごろに止まります。朝5時まで電車はありません。

68
00:07:09,759 --> 00:07:18,423
[News] 夜遅くまで働く人や、外国からの旅行者にとって、これは不便です。

69
00:07:19,124 --> 00:07:26,396
[News] まず、金曜日と土曜日の夜だけ、試してみる予定です。

70
00:07:27,096 --> 00:07:32,879
[News] 使う路線は、山手線と中央線です。

71
00:07:33,579 --> 00:07:42,172
[News] でも、夜の間に線路の点検をしているので、安全の問題があります。

72
00:07:42,872 --> 00:07:54,103
[News] 東京都の担当者は、「安全が一番大切です。よく調べてから決めます」と言いました。

73
00:07:54,803 --> 00:07:57,540
[Host] Quiz time. Three questions.

74
00:07:58,139 --> 00:08:05,028
[Slow] 今、東京の電車は何時に止まりますか？

75
00:08:05,827 --> 00:08:09,620
[Host] What time do Tokyo trains stop? Around midnight.

76
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:15,620
[Slow] まず、何曜日に試しますか？

77
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:20,403
[Host] What days will they try first? Friday and Saturday.

78
00:08:21,004 --> 00:08:25,684
[Slow] 何が一番大切ですか？

79
00:08:26,483 --> 00:08:55,427
[Host] What is the most important thing? Safety. Now let's do a vocabulary recap. 'Densha' means train. 'Tomarimasu' means stops. 'Hataraku' means to work. 'Fuben' means inconvenient. 'Yotei' means plan or schedule. 'Anzen' means safety. 'Mondai' means problem. 'Ichiban' means number one, the most. 'Taisetsu' means important. And 'kimemasu' means to decide.

80
00:08:56,028 --> 00:09:08,412
[Host] That's today's Japanese Daily News. Full transcript with furigana at podcast.jpnotes.dev. Subscribe on Apple Podcasts or Spotify. See you tomorrow.

81
00:09:09,011 --> 00:09:11,412
[News] また明日。
